--- body ---

## Context

Author, 2026-09-15, having asked whether a ticket existed for this and been
shown 0792's deferred section:

> J'en ai marre du bouton. C'est un checkbox "Pause indexing". Qui commence
> checked, et devient controlable quand l'indexing peut être lancé (boot wait,
> sync). Quand il est auto-checked, ça dit pourquoi. Quand il est unchecked, ça
> dit la phase.

### What the ruling fixes

`#sdt-switch` is a `<button>` whose visible text names the **action to take** --
"Turn indexing on" / "Turn indexing off" -- so it inverts in the same instant it
is clicked. Ticket 0790 patched the *announcement* (an `aria-label` carrying a
state-then-action sentence, `switch-turned-on` / `switch-turned-off`) and left
the widget alone, because the widget was the open question 0792 then deferred.

A checkbox carries state natively. Checked/unchecked *is* the state, the label
never inverts, and the 0790 `aria-label` workaround can go with it: the reason
that attribute exists is that a button's name had to do a job a checkbox's
`checked` does for free.

It also puts the UI on SPEC.md's own vocabulary. R22 is named **"Pause"**; the
control that satisfies it has until now said "Turn indexing off".

### Two corrections this ruling makes to closed ticket 0792

1. **Pause/Play was rejected there; the label is now the author's.** 0792's
   ground was that `launch-worker` "cannot be paused once a file has started",
   and that pause implies session-scoped state where this is a remembered
   preference. The first half becomes a **design constraint on one string, not
   a veto**: checking the box ends admissions and the document already in
   flight finishes, exactly as `disarmSDTSitter` already behaves ("nothing is
   cancelled; the sitter simply stops asking"). The line beside the box must
   say so while it is true, and `RELEASE-NOTES.md` must not promise an instant
   stop. No scheduler or admission semantics change anywhere in this ticket.
   The second half is answered by the reading adopted below: the preference
   stays remembered.

2. **Drop `role="switch"`.** 0792's recommendation was
   `<input type="checkbox" role="switch">`, chosen when the control was still
   imagined as a slider. With "Pause indexing" as the label it is a checkbox;
   the ARIA role would only make a screen reader say "switch" about a thing the
   label calls a checkbox. A plain `<input type="checkbox">` sits strictly
   inside 0792's costed option 1: no Lit, no `chrome://` coupling, no
   stylesheet, no `<style>` element in a window built from twelve inline
   `style.cssText` sites, and Gecko keeps the native widget, the focus ring and
   the forced-colours rendering.

### Settled: no remembered state, start on as soon as possible

Author, 2026-09-15, asked which of the two readings below he meant and
answering with a third:

> Je préfère ne pas avoir d'état donc aucune préférence mémorisée. D'ailleurs
> ce n'est marqué nulle part "Préférence mémorisée". On commence allumé dès que
> possible.

Neither reading this ticket first offered, then. There is no remembered answer
to survive or to revoke. The box is checked and non-interactive while indexing
cannot be launched, the line says why, and the moment it can be launched
**indexing starts**: the box unchecks itself and nobody is asked. Checking it
afterwards pauses for this session, and the next Zotero start begins indexing
again.

The two readings that are now moot, kept in one line each so nobody re-derives
them: the pref survives and the box reflects it (what this ticket first
adopted), or the box starts checked every session and the user unchecks it each
time (which would have revoked ticket 0742). The ruling discards the mechanism
both were arguing about.

**His second sentence, checked.** It is very nearly true, and the exception
argues for the ruling rather than against it. Exactly one string says the
choice persists -- `launch-details`, "This answer is remembered." -- and it
lives in the launch modal, which is shown at most once per profile and
deliberately never returns (ticket 0742). So the only statement that the
preference is remembered sits on the one surface designed never to be seen
twice, while the window the user actually consults says nothing about it. State
that nothing on screen admits to is the thing the ruling removes.

### This ruling amends R22, and the implementation must not ship around it

R22 reads: "There MUST be one obvious way to stop all background work, and it
MUST hold across restarts", and gives its own reason -- "background work that
resumes on its own after a reboot was never stopped, only interrupted."

A pause that ends at the next Zotero start does not hold across restarts.
**R22's second clause is what this ruling changes**, and SPEC.md must say so
before the behaviour ships: a requirement that still claims the opposite of the
running code is worse than either. Amending R22 is Action 9 and it gates the
implementation rather than trailing it.

R22's first clause is untouched. One obvious way survives, and a checkbox
labelled "Pause indexing" is a more obvious way than a button that names the
inverse action.

### Which states check the box by themselves

The ruling names boot wait and sync as the conditions that hold the control.
Read with "when it is unchecked, it says the phase", that puts the blocked
phases on the *unchecked* side: `cpu-busy`, `low-memory`, `low-disk`,
`storage-unavailable`, `resources-unavailable` and `native-worker-busy` are
phases, they already have decided labels in `SDT_PHASE_LABELS`, and the user
has not paused anything when one of them holds. Auto-checked is reserved for
the two states where the user's intent cannot change the outcome at all.

Keeping a transient `cpu-busy` off the checkbox also keeps the control from
flickering checked and unchecked under ordinary load, which is the 10 Hz defect
in slower clothes -- the thing 0792's own invariants exist to prevent.

### The table the implementation owes

| Sitter state | box | interactive | the line beside it says |
|---|---|---|---|
| boot wait -- the sitter cannot yet be armed | checked | no | why: Zotero is still starting |
| sync in progress | checked | no | why: waiting for sync to finish |
| user paused this session (`phase === 'switched-off'`) | checked | yes | `switch-off`, minus any claim of persistence |
| checked, a document still in flight | checked | yes | finishing the current document, then stopping |
| `census` | unchecked | yes | `switch-on-census` |
| `draining` | unchecked | yes | `switch-on-draining` |
| `extracting` | unchecked | yes | `switch-on-extracting` |
| a blocked phase | unchecked | yes | that phase's `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` entry |
| idle | unchecked | yes | `describeSDTIdleLine`'s counts |
| removal noticed (0781) | checked | no | `vanished-message`, unchanged |

Two rows are new work and not merely a rewiring of what exists: **sync** has no
gate anywhere in the plugin today (see Actions 4), and **checked with a document
in flight** has no string today.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js`
  - `~2639-2660` -- where the control is built: `sdt-switch-row`, the
    `sdt-switch-state` span, and `element('button', 'sdt-switch')` with its
    `type=button`, its `white-space: nowrap` sizing note and its click listener.
    The flex `space-between` comment explains why the button is pinned right;
    a checkbox's box + label is a different shape and that reasoning must be
    re-read, not inherited.
  - `~2322-2356` -- the per-render block: `#sdt-switch-state`'s text,
    `announceSDTTransition`, `switch-turn-on`/`switch-turn-off` and the 0790
    `aria-label`. `const off = s.phase === 'switched-off'` is the polarity that
    inverts here: **checked <=> not indexing**.
  - `~1651-1660` `describeSDTSwitchLine` -- already says the phase when on and
    `switch-off` when off; it gains the two new cases.
  - `~1672-1690` `describeSDTSwitchKind` -- the announcement identity. It must
    stay digits-free, or a moving count reaches the live region.
  - `~1014-1035` `SDT_PHASE_LABELS`, `~718` `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES`.
  - `~1052-1070` `readSDTSwitch` / `writeSDTSwitch`; `~1140-1150`
    `toggleSDTSwitch` (writes the pref FIRST, deliberately).
  - `~1104-1135` `armSDTSitter` / `disarmSDTSitter`.
  - `~3436-3459` -- the startup launch decision, where `enabled === null`
    reaches `askSDTLaunch` and where `armSDTSitter()` or `disarmSDTSitter()` is
    chosen. This is where "boot wait" has to become a state the render can read
    rather than a gap before `alive = true`.
  - `~354-373` -- the string table: `switch-on-prefix`, `switch-off`,
    `switch-turn-on`, `switch-turn-off`, `switch-turned-on`, `switch-turned-off`.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs` -- the dialog suite, drives a stub document and
  pins the window's layer order.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`, `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` -- the
  scheduler suite asks `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` about every phase it can enumerate.
- `verification/probes/sdt-sitter-harness/bootstrap.js` -- already asserts a
  bound checkbox label for the diagnostics pref; the same pattern applies.
- `verification/probes/sdt_panel_keyboard.js`, `run_panel_keyboard.py` -- focus
  and keyboard assertions.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/RELEASE-NOTES.md`, `DESIGN-NOTES.md`, `SPEC.md` (R22).

## Actions

1. Replace `element('button', 'sdt-switch')` with an `<input type="checkbox">`
   plus a bound `<label>`, unstyled, no `role`. Bind by `for`/`id` so the label
   text is clickable and is the accessible name. Re-read the row's flex
   reasoning rather than porting it: the comment that pins the button right was
   written about a two-word button whose text changes length, and neither
   clause is true of a fixed "Pause indexing" label.
2. Invert the polarity at the one place it is computed. `checked` is
   `!indexing`, derived from the sitter's phase exactly as `off` is today --
   "reread from the sitter's own phase rather than from the pref: the two
   agree, and a disagreement shows here instead of hiding" is a property worth
   keeping.
3. Make boot wait a readable state. Today the window does not exist before
   `alive = true`, so "checked and non-interactive during boot wait" needs the
   render to be able to distinguish *not yet armable* from *user paused*. Do
   not invent a phase name that announces per document.
3b. Remove the persisted answer, all of it or none of it. `readSDTSwitch` stops
   being consulted at startup (`bootstrap.js:3436`) and the startup path arms
   unconditionally once indexing is launchable; `askSDTLaunch` and its five
   strings (`launch-title`, `launch-question`, `launch-conditions`,
   `launch-details`, `launch-yes`/`launch-no`) go with it, because a prompt
   whose only job is to write a preference that no longer exists has nothing to
   ask. Decide explicitly what happens to `extensions.sdt-pack-sitter.enabled`
   itself -- a pref left written but unread is exactly the invisible state the
   ruling is against -- and to `bootstrap.js:3560`, which writes `false` into it
   on uninstall.
   Half of this is worse than none: dropping the modal while still reading the
   pref leaves a machine that obeys an answer the user can no longer give.
4. Add the sync condition. **No Zotero-sync gate exists in the plugin today** --
   grep for it before building: `sync` appears in `bootstrap.js` only in a
   not-indexed detail string and in unrelated prose. The candidate reading,
   from the concurrent session's live work on tickets 0795/0796, is
   `Zotero.Sync.Runner.syncInProgress`. **It is a candidate, not a fact**: it
   must be sampled twice, once while a sync is running and once at rest, and
   adopted only if the two readings differ. A key identical in both states does
   not discriminate and must not be used -- that probe is exactly this ticket's
   positive control, and the reading is taken from a live Zotero window, since
   nothing offline can settle it.
5. Retire `switch-turn-on` / `switch-turn-off` / `switch-turned-on` /
   `switch-turned-off` and add: the checkbox label, the boot-wait reason, the
   sync reason, and the in-flight "finishing the current document" line. Delete
   the retired ids rather than leaving them unreferenced.
6. Drop the 0790 `aria-label` on this control and say in the commit why it is
   safe to drop: the defect it patched was a button label that named the
   inverse action, and a checkbox has no such label.
7. Update `RELEASE-NOTES.md` and `DESIGN-NOTES.md`. The release note must state
   that checking the box stops new work and lets the current document finish --
   the promise 0792 refused to let Pause/Play make.
8. Bump the manifest to 0.4.18 or later. PR #585 already holds 0.4.17, and
   `bench/check_sitter_version.py` rule 3 reddens when two builds answer to one
   number. Two mechanics of that guard, reported by the session that just hit
   both and confirmed here against the script:
   - **It hashes bytes, not behaviour.** The payload is digested with
     `hashlib.sha256` over the files `build_sdt_sitter.DELIVERED` names, and the
     invariant is that no two DIFFERENT payloads share a version. A
     comment-only edit to `bootstrap.js` is a different payload. Expect to bump
     for a change that alters nothing at runtime, and do not read a redden here
     as evidence that behaviour moved.
   - **NOT-RUN is not green.** The forge leg reports on its own line and answers
     NOT-RUN when it cannot enumerate the open pull requests -- an HTTP 504 did
     it once -- because "a gate that cannot look must never answer as" green
     (its own words, `check_sitter_version.py:85`). A version another open
     branch has already claimed would read as unclaimed in that state. Re-run
     it; never take the NOT-RUN line for a pass.
9. Amend SPEC.md R22's second clause, in the same PR as the behaviour. The
   pause no longer holds across restarts, by the author's ruling; R22 currently
   requires that it does, and gives a rationale that must be rewritten rather
   than deleted, since the reasoning it states ("background work that resumes
   on its own after a reboot was never stopped") is precisely what the new
   behaviour does. Say what replaces it: the pause is for this session, and
   starting on is the default the author chose.

## Test

Red first, in `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs`, against the stub document:

- The control is an `input[type=checkbox]` with a bound label, and its
  `checked` is true when the phase is `switched-off` and false when the phase
  is `census`. Today there is no checkbox at all, so this is red by absence.
- **The control that matters**: assert the box is `disabled` during boot wait
  and `enabled` under a blocked phase (`cpu-busy`). A test that only asserts
  "checked when off" passes against a checkbox wired to the pref alone and
  would miss the entire ruling. Both arms must be shown to fail before the fix
  -- a suite where the blocked-phase arm is green by construction is the defect
  ticket 0787 recorded in its own words: focusable and reachable were never the
  same claim.
- The announcement kind stays digits-free across a count change.

## Verification

- [ ] A test asserting `disabled` during boot wait fails before the change
- [ ] A test asserting the box is NOT checked under `cpu-busy` fails before the
      change
- [ ] `node --test tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs` and the other
      `tests/sdt_sitter_*.mjs` suites pass
- [ ] `make sitter-mutants` passes -- both probes anchor on this region
- [ ] The two sync probe readings taken, active and at rest, and shown to differ
      before `syncInProgress` (or whatever replaces it) is wired to the control
- [ ] `make sitter-smoke SITTER_SMOKE_ZOTERO=~/.local/Zotero_linux-x86_64/zotero`
      run against a real Zotero, and the checkbox read in a live window: what a
      screen reader announces for it, and that the native widget survives a
      forced-colours setting. 0792's rendering claims are all read from source
      and none is observed; this ticket must not repeat that.
- [ ] `grep` shows the retired string ids are gone, not merely unreferenced --
      the four switch ids and, per Action 3b, the five launch ids
- [ ] SPEC.md R22 amended in the same PR as the behaviour, and no other SPEC
      requirement still asserts a pause that survives a restart
- [ ] Quitting Zotero with the box checked and restarting begins indexing again,
      read in a live window, not inferred from the code

## Invariants

- Admission policy, thresholds, and what gets extracted do not change.
- `disarmSDTSitter`'s semantics do not change: nothing is cancelled, the sitter
  stops asking, `alive` stays true so the window and the control remain.
- R22's FIRST clause holds: one obvious way to stop all background work, and no
  second switch appears anywhere. Its second clause is amended by Action 9, not
  quietly broken -- the pause is session-scoped by ruling, and SPEC must say so
  in the same PR.
- No state the window does not admit to. The ruling's reason is that a
  remembered answer was stated only in a modal shown once per profile, so any
  replacement that persists something invisibly -- a pref read but not shown, a
  cache that restores the paused state -- reintroduces exactly what it removed.
- Toggling off then on must keep forcing a fresh census: `stop()` burns the
  epoch, `start()` sets `reconciliationPending`, and the census that follows is
  what moves attachments out of `missing-source` once their files have landed.
  That much is measured (see the log entry of this date). A checkbox that only
  writes a pref, or that debounces its own transitions, would take it away.
  It is **not** an escape hatch from the drain starvation of ticket 0796 --
  also measured, and the opposite of what this ticket's earlier log entry
  claimed -- so nothing here may be designed as if the control could rescue a
  starved pump.
- No new phase announces per document (the 10 Hz defect).
- The count in the line beside the checkbox and the count in the "Not
  indexed" heading it points the reader at must go on agreeing. They are
  computed by two independent routes (`SDT_STATUS_CLASSES` versus
  `collectSDTNotIndexed`) and PR #585 has just brought them into line by
  dropping `no-attachment` from the heading; a rewrite of
  `describeSDTIdleLine` that re-adds an unattached population to the
  sentence would silently undo that.
- Ticket 0781's removal notice still wins over every other reading in the row.
- The control stays focusable. Ticket 0787's ruling stands and is NOT reopened
  here: plugin toolbar buttons are outside Zotero's keyboard navigation chain,
  that is upstream's gap, and nothing in this ticket works around it. The
  checkbox lives in the dialog, which is a different document from the toolbar.

## Exit criteria

The dialog's on/off control is a checkbox labelled "Pause indexing" that is
checked when nothing is being indexed and unchecked when something is; it is
non-interactive exactly while the user's choice cannot change the outcome, and
in that state the line beside it says why; when it is interactive and unchecked
that line says the phase. Nothing about the choice is remembered: the next
Zotero start indexes as soon as it can, and SPEC.md R22 says that rather than
the opposite. The behaviour is demonstrated by a suite that was red on both the
boot-wait and the blocked-phase arms, and read once in a live Zotero window.
